WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Today we'll be going over the basics of how to use Sympheny.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.000
So we'll do a very simple project just to illustrate how to use the app.

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Sympheny is a really flexible software, but there is a lot of data available.

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.000
Here i'm selecting solar panels in the database.

5
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.000
We can see that there's quite a lot of technologies and quite a lot of options just for solar panels.

6
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.000
There we have our annual solar irradiance profile.

7
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.000
So the energy diagram will be updated every time you add something to it.

8
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.000
All of the items on our dashboard are interactive.

9
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:42.000
You can view values, move things around, filter out components, and download the data table with all of the data.

10
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.000
This is what you will see once you've created a new account.

11
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.000
You have access to an example project.

12
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Let's start with creating our first project.

13
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.000
We give it a name.

14
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.000
This will be a demo on the topic of a data center.

15
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.000
We can Give it a location.

16
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.000
This will help you find your projects when you have many.

17
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Now that we have our project, let's open it up.

18
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.000
For now it's empty.

19
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.000
We have a few options here.

20
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.000
We can edit it, of course.

21
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.000
We can also share it with our colleagues.

22
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.000
We'll start by adding an image to illustrate the project.

23
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Here we go.

24
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.000
Let's add an analysis.

25
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.000
Scenarios can be used to compare different situations.

26
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:39.000
In this case, we want a first scenario to be about solar power only.

27
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.000
So we'll do a very simple project today just to illustrate how to use the app.

28
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.000
We click into the scenario.

29
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Now we are inside the scenario editor.

30
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.000
We can see a number of steps up here.

31
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.000
First let's change the currency.

32
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Maybe for this project we're using euros.

33
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.000
We need to have a stage.

34
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Stages are meant to represent faces in our project.

35
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:15.000
These are used mostly for very large-scale projects when we are planning the entire heating system of a district, including network, over decades.

36
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.000
Let's say 20 year.

37
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:21.000
Starting out, we can set the interest rate and the inflation as zero.

38
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.000
This will help us interpret results.

39
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.000
The next step we need to add a hub.

40
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.000
So a hub is a geographical location.

41
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:39.000
We can use the search function.

42
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:52.000
We can change the overlay.

43
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.000
I'll simply draw the outline.

44
00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:02.000
This is just to kind of anchor the project geographically and show the stakeholders what we're talking about.

45
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.000
We can also with add-ons extract information directly from the map.

46
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:17.000
If we want to have surface and heating system for example of a building or a group of buildings that's possible to get directly from the map.

47
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.000
A hub is a geographical location.

48
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.000
If we draw more of them, we can connect them with networks.

49
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:31.000
This is necessary when we're modeling, for example, district heating or electrical systems, or you have different buildings that are electrically connected.

50
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.000
A hub can be a single building or, in this case, a group of buildings.

51
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Next step is energy carriers.

52
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.000
We'll add these energy carriers automatically by going directly to supply technology.

53
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.000
and adding some from the database.

54
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.000
Sympheny is a really flexible software, but there is a lot of data available.

55
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.000
Here i'm selecting solar panels in the database.

56
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.000
We can see that there is quite a lot of technologies and quite a lot of options just for solar panels.

57
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.000
I'll take the larger array.

58
00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.000
These will be cheaper per kilowatt.

59
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:02.000
And here we have missing energy carriers.

60
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:03.000
Do you want to add them?

61
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:05.000
Yes.

62
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:07.000
So we're adding solar irradiance and electricity.

63
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:12.000
So we can see that this already has all of the information we need.

64
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.000
This is the efficiency of this technology.

65
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:24.000
So this is the sun hitting the panel and the electricity produced overall is 18%, including inverter efficiency, inclination, shading, everything.

66
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.000
We have some technical parameters.

67
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.000
We can set the capacity or the production or other limits for the technology.

68
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.000
In this case, we'll just let Sympheny size and optimize this solar field.

69
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.000
We also have all of the cost parameters already entered.

70
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.000
This comes from our database.

71
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:44.000
We can edit anything we want to or we can leave it as it is.

72
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:49.000
We also have the possibility to enter values for embedded emissions.

73
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.000
Okay, so now we have our solar panels.

74
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:57.000
If we go back to energy carriers, we can see that electricity and solar roof has been automatically entered.

75
00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.000
If you want, you can change the color or the name.

76
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.000
For this example, we only have one energy demand.

77
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.000
It will be electricity, so electricity that the facility uses.

78
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:09.000
This is a bit of an odd one.

79
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.000
It's not a standard building, it's a data center.

80
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:18.000
And we can see that in our database we have a lot of options, but we do not have data centers.

81
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:26.000
We can still try out something that maybe sounds close, industry warehouse, and see where this will take us.

82
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.000
Here we have a value per square meter coming out for the norms for this kind of building.

83
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.000
In this case, this is not really correct because it's not the one we want to model.

84
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:39.000
So from the project, we have a value of 1.2 GWh of electricity per year.

85
00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.000
We'll enter that here.

86
00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:44.000
Some information on where this data comes from.

87
00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.000
Save and close.

88
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:51.000
And we see that there is quite a lot of variation based on weekends.

89
00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:54.000
Maybe we're not very happy with this one and we want to change it.

90
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.000
we can actually upload our own.

91
00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:05.000
So upload profile, go to downloads, take this one.

92
00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:08.000
It's been updated to something a bit more realistic.

93
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.000
There is quite a number of options here.

94
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:16.000
We can smooth out the profile if we enter a number between 1 and 20 here.

95
00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.000
So there is a lot you can do with Symfony.

96
00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:25.000
This is a simple Excel file with 8,760 values.

97
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:28.000
You can grab it.

98
00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.000
We can open it on the screen to look at the format.

99
00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:34.000
There we see the format.

100
00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:42.000
So it's a simple Excel file with two columns.

101
00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.000
We're also going to add an on-site resource for solar power.

102
00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.000
You can see that we have two options.

103
00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.000
We can add it from the map, so to simulate virtually any location.

104
00:06:53.000 --> 00:07:00.000
But for this case, since we're in Geneva, we're simply going to select here the ones that are available in our database.

105
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.000
So let's say that it's 10,000 square meter of roof surface available.

106
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:09.000
And we're only going to simulate one orientation.

107
00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:14.000
Here it's going to be 15 degrees east.

108
00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:21.000
And if we click the View button, there we have our annual solar irradiance profile.

109
00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:29.000
Okay, if you've been paying attention, in every step we have some things that are happening below here and on top on top we have the editor for that step.

110
00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:34.000
And below we have a map that i can hide and also an energy diagram.

111
00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:38.000
So the energy diagram will be updated every time you add something to it.

112
00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:48.000
And here we can see on the left the onsite resource, in the middle the technology, and on the right the electricity demand.

113
00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.000
Let's add the grid to finish modeling this problem.

114
00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:56.000
So we'll add grid electricity.

115
00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:03.000
This is electricity that can be purchased at let's say 15 cents per kWh.

116
00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:09.000
We can also give a capacity cost in euro per kilowatt per year.

117
00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:12.000
We have a number of technical parameters.

118
00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.000
We can limit this on a per hour basis.

119
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.000
The tariff can also be an hourly profile.

120
00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.000
We also have parameters for the environment for emissions.

121
00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:26.000
Let's add here 0.1 kilogram of CO2 per kWh.

122
00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:30.000
Let's do the same for the export.

123
00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.000
So this will be surplus PV.

124
00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:35.000
It's also electricity that we can sell.

125
00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:43.000
This is positive value and it means we're going to earn money when we're exporting this electricity.

126
00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:48.000
Before we execute and solve this problem, important to take a look at the energy diagram.

127
00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:54.000
We can see that there is no loose ends and our very, very simple problem looks complete.

128
00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:59.000
So we go to execute, give it a name, select the scenario.

129
00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:05.000
Here are different parameters that can be changed to make the solution more accurate or faster.

130
00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:11.000
You can see that we have choices between different objectives that we can use.

131
00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:14.000
Once we have the results, of an execution that will show up here.

132
00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.000
Here's where all the history of the execution will be displayed.

133
00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:20.000
You can also access them from here.

134
00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:26.000
We could download the Excel file of all of the parameters used to simulate the scenario.

135
00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:30.000
We can also download all of the results in the Excel format.

136
00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.000
We can view the parameters or we can go on our interactive dashboard.

137
00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:41.000
We can head to all stages and display the Pareto front for this very simple problem.

138
00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:46.000
Both solutions are virtually the same.

139
00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:53.000
Once you select a solution, a station, a hub, you get to the detailed view for this result.

140
00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:56.000
All of the items on our dashboard are interactive.

141
00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:04.000
You can view values, move things around, filter out components and download the data table with all of the data.

142
00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:11.000
This Sankey diagram of energy flows is available for every month of the year.

143
00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:18.000
The energy diagram now contains only the technologies that are part of the optimal solution.

144
00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:23.000
We have the solar field, which is about 1700 kilowatt.

145
00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:29.000
That is about what we expect to have for a field that covers 10,000 square meter.

146
00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:32.000
We have an efficiency of 18%.

147
00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:38.000
We know that on average in Europe we get about 1 kilowatt per square meter of ton power.

148
00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:57.000
We multiply that 18% by 10,000, we get about 1,800.

149
00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:03.000
Let's head back into the project and we are going to create a scenario with a battery.

150
00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:07.000
So copy this scenario, rename it.

151
00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:14.000
This will be PV plus 100 kWh battery.

152
00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:16.000
Now head into the scenario.

153
00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:20.000
All of the parameters we set before have been carried over.

154
00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:33.000
So all we need to do is just to add the battery or add it from the database.

155
00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:43.000
I'll rename it battery 100 kWh, because for this one we will not optimize the capacity, we will specify the capacity 100 kWh.

156
00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:50.000
And if we specify the capacity and that can be installed, then it can still not be installed, but must be installed.

157
00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:56.000
So for this scenario we're not giving any wiggle room, the cost parameters leave it the same.

158
00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:05.000
Now that's done, we can go directly to execution, we see that the battery has been added here on the diagram.

159
00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:14.000
We can execute again.

160
00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:17.000
Okay, execution is done.

161
00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:22.000
View results.

162
00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:28.000
Again, we can check out the Pareto font.

163
00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:32.000
This time we can see that the lifecycle cost has been slightly reduced.

164
00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:40.000
The battery saved us about 30K Euro.

165
00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:41.000
The results look rather similar.

166
00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:52.000
The energy stored in the battery is not huge compared to the GWh that's been consumed by the system.

167
00:12:52.000 --> 00:13:02.000
What we can see is the state of charge of the battery that wasn't there before.

168
00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:07.000
Here we see how the battery is used on a daily basis.

169
00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:11.875
The self-sufficiency has been somewhat increased.

